# Erdős Problem #99 — correlated stopped-train surplus

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Repository base:** `bb363adf`
**State version:** `2026-09-14.audit.51`
**Target:** Target C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch
**Status:** proof-only candidate depending on the AG42 `eta_grid=0.00042` refinement; theorem-state authority unchanged

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

This note couples the all-stopped-train atomic gap to the exact split between
rhombic and singleton stopped trains.  The sharpened constants use the proof-only AG42
refinement `eta_grid=0.00042`; therefore every `0.00042`-dependent conclusion below
inherits AG42's awaiting-audit status.  Let `T` be the full stopped-train count of
§142, `T_R=B_4/2` the non-singleton rhombic-train count, `S=T-T_R` the singleton
count, and

\[
\sigma_R=B_4-4\sqrt{R_4},\qquad
\rho_{rec}=b+4f+Q-B_4.
\]

The main result is

\[
\boxed{\liminf {\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R-q\over D}\ge0.00084.}
\tag{CS.1}
\]

Together with the canonical difference-body bound

\[
\liminf {q\over D}\ge2\rho,
\qquad \rho=\cos\rho=0.7390851332151606\ldots,
\tag{CS.2}
\]

this gives

\[
\boxed{\liminf {\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R\over D}
\ge2\rho+0.00084=1.4790102664303213\ldots}
\tag{CS.3}
\]

and hence

\[
\boxed{\liminf {\max\{\rho_{rec},\sigma_R\}\over D}
\ge\rho+0.00042=0.7395051332151606\ldots .}
\tag{CS.4}
\]

Inserting (CS.3) into the exact post-endpoint identity raises the proof-only full-core
surplus floor to

\[
\boxed{\liminf\mu_D\ge
\mu_{TC}+{\rho\over5}+0.000084
=1.785463210824532\ldots .}
\tag{CS.5}
\]

This does not close C-II because no universal `mu_D` ceiling below (CS.5) is currently
available.  It does remove the former near-floor scalar region and forces one of two
large geometric mechanisms.

## 1. Exact stopped-train bookkeeping

The §142 stopped-train decomposition includes singleton positive-contact edges.  Every
train containing a strict rhombus has two strict-rhombus boundary ends, so

\[
T=T_R+S,
\qquad B_4=2T_R.
\tag{CS.6}
\]

The full endpoint handshake is

\[
b+4f+Q=2T.
\tag{CS.7}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{\rho_{rec}=b+4f+Q-B_4=2S.}
\tag{CS.8}
\]

Since `sigma_R=2T_R-4sqrt(R_4)`,

\[
\boxed{\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R=2T-4\sqrt{R_4}.}
\tag{CS.9}
\]

Equivalently, using `T-q=2f-k`, the old train-boundary slack

\[
\tau_D=q-k+2f-2\sqrt{R_4}=T-2\sqrt{R_4}
\]

satisfies the exact finite identity

\[
\boxed{2\tau_D=\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R.}
\tag{CS.10}
\]

## 2. Audited §168 preserves a loaded-hull term

The audited §168 argument, with the proof-only AG42 quantitative refinement, gives

\[
\boxed{\liminf {T-q/2\over D}
\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00042,}
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\tag{CS.11}
\]

Canonical full-core rhombic saturation gives

\[
{\sqrt{R_4}\over D}\to\sqrt{\Delta_2}.
\tag{CS.12}
\]

Subtracting the convergent `2sqrt(R_4)/D` term from (CS.11),

\[
\liminf {T-2\sqrt{R_4}-q/2\over D}\ge0.00042.
\tag{CS.13}
\]

Using (CS.10) and multiplying by two proves (CS.1).  The crucial point is that the
full `q` term survives; this is far stronger than the separate universal
`sigma_R/D>=0.00084` bound.

## 3. Difference-body density makes the correlation macroscopic

Audited §59 proves

\[
\liminf {h\over D}\ge2\rho=1.4781702664303213\ldots
\tag{CS.14}
\]

and canonically `q>=h`.  Combining this with (CS.1) gives (CS.3), and
`max(x,y)>=(x+y)/2` gives (CS.4).

Thus every sufficiently large full-core survivor has, up to `o(D)`, at least one of:

1. **singleton branch**
   \[
   \rho_{rec}\ge(\rho+0.00042-o(1))D,
   \]
   hence
   \[
   S\ge(\rho/2+0.00021-o(1))D
   =0.3697525666075803\ldots D-o(D);
   \]
2. **rhombic non-Mantel branch**
   \[
   \sigma_R\ge(\rho+0.00042-o(1))D.
   \]

## 4. Explicit full-core `mu_D` floor

The exact post-endpoint decomposition is

\[
\begin{aligned}
\mu_DD={}&{2P_F+2\sqrt{R_4}\over5}
+m_B+{2\over5}(Q-f)+{2\over5}(b-P_F)\\
&+{1\over10}\rho_{rec}+{1\over10}\sigma_R-2c_I-4.
\end{aligned}
\tag{CS.15}
\]

Here `P_F/D->pi`, `R_4/D^2->Delta_2`, `c_I/D->0`, and the three remaining defect
terms are nonnegative.  Therefore (CS.3) gives

\[
\boxed{\liminf\mu_D\ge
{2\pi+2\sqrt{\Delta_2}\over5}+{2\rho+0.00084\over10}
=1.785463210824532\ldots .}
\tag{CS.16}
\]

The improvement over `mu_TC` is `0.147901026643032...`, so this is not a perturbative
sharpening.

Earlier near-floor abstract scalar witnesses did not encode the audited §168
correlation between the full stopped-train count and `q`.  Future full-stack scalar
regressions must include (CS.1) or an equivalent form; witnesses with
`mu<1.785459...` are not compatible with the complete currently available
canonical/audited-working/proof-only input stack.

## 5. The large-Mantel side is genuinely quadratic

On the second branch put `c_*=rho+0.00042`.  With

\[
M_R={T_R^2\over4}-R_4,
\]

the exact rationalization is

\[
M_R={\sigma_R(T_R+2\sqrt{R_4})\over8}
={\sigma_R\over8}
\left(4\sqrt{R_4}+{\sigma_R\over2}\right).
\tag{CS.17}
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{\liminf {M_R\over D^2}\ge
{c_*\over8}\left(4\sqrt{\Delta_2}+{c_*\over2}\right)
=0.3862993446794404\ldots .}
\tag{CS.18}
\]

This is a macroscopic fraction of the Mantel capacity, qualitatively stronger than the
small universal deficit in the preceding rhombic-train note.

## 6. Correlated endpoint-defect floor

The full stopped-train handshake also turns the sharpened all-train theorem into a
large direct boundary/interface statement.  Put

\[
E_{exc}:=Q+4f.
\]

Then

\[
(b-P_F)+E_{exc}=2T-P_F.
\tag{CS.19}
\]

Using the sharpened all-train bound

\[
{T-q/2\over D}\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00042-o(1),
\]

together with `q/D>=2rho-o(1)` and `P_F/D->pi`, we obtain

\[
\boxed{
\liminf { (b-P_F)+(Q+4f) \over D}
\ge
2\rho+4\sqrt{\Delta_2}-\pi+0.00084
=2.1466688402963574\ldots .
}
\tag{CS.20}
\]

This is a full stopped-train endpoint-defect floor.  It is much larger than the
rhombic-boundary-only baseline
`4sqrt(Delta_2)-pi=0.667658573866...`, because it preserves the loaded-hull term from
§168 rather than discarding singleton trains.

It does not by itself close C-II: the theorem stack has no sufficiently small universal
upper bound on `Q+4f`.  It does, however, show that any survivor has more than
`2.1466D` combined metric outer detour and exceptional-face perimeter.

## 7. Live closure targets

The full-core branch is now reduced to two sharply different geometric mechanisms:

- about `0.36974D` or more singleton positive-contact edges belonging to no strict
  rhombus; or
- rhombic isoperimetric slack about `0.73949D` or more, hence the macroscopic
  quadratic crossing deficit (CS.18).

These should be charged separately.  Singleton trains are actual defect-network edges;
large rhombic Mantel deficit is missing quadratic crossing capacity among rhombic
pseudosegments.  Merging them back into one scalar discards the new information.
